<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benedictines</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/529">529</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Order of Saint Benedict, O.S.B., Black Monks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Benedictines, officially the Order of Saint Benedict, are a mainly contemplative monastic order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> for men and women who follow the Rule of Saint Benedict. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-of-nursia">Benedict of Nursia</a> established the monastery at Subiaco in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> c. 529 and later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Abbey of Monte Cassino</a>, though there is no evidence he meant to found an order. By the ninth century the Benedictine rule had become the standard form of monastic life across most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. The order is a collection of autonomous monasteries, represented since 1893 by the Benedictine Confederation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Copies of the Rule survived the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, about 580, after which the monks fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, a move that probably helped to spread knowledge of Benedictine monasticism. Around 594 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a> spoke favourably of the Rule. In sixth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> it was used alongside other rules, and in Gaul and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> it gradually supplemented the stricter Irish rule introduced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbanus">Columbanus</a>. Largely through the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-of-aniane">Benedict of Aniane</a>, it became the rule of choice across the Carolingian empire.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cluny-abbey">Cluny Abbey</a> was founded in 910 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-duke-of-aquitaine">William I, Duke of Aquitaine</a>, and was noted for its strict adherence to the Rule; its abbot was the superior of all its daughter houses. Romuald began a reform in 980 that produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camaldolese">Camaldolese</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cistercians">Cistercians</a> branched off in 1098. Monastic scriptoria flourished from the ninth to the twelfth century, and a scribe might spend six hours a day copying. From the late twelfth century the mendicant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> and Dominicans, not bound to one place as Benedictines were by their vow of stability, answered better to growing towns, and the practice of appointing lay commendatory abbots drained monastic assets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Despite the name of an order, the Benedictines are a collection of autonomous monasteries and convents, with no superior general. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a> created the Benedictine Confederation by the brief Summum semper on 12 July 1893, with an Abbot Primate elected to represent it, seated at the Primatial Abbey of Sant'Anselmo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Houses differ in dedication: Solesmes in Gregorian chant, Stanbrook Abbey in books and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, the Olivetans, established by Bernardo Tolomei in 1313, in their own tradition.</p><p>Candidates take vows of stability, of conversatio morum, commonly understood as a monastic manner of life, and of obedience to the superior. A fixed timetable, the horarium, divides the day among prayer, work, meals, reading and sleep, under the motto Ora et Labora, &quot;pray and work&quot;. There is no vow of silence, but hours of strict silence are kept. The Rule's forty-eighth chapter prescribes extensive holy reading, and monastic libraries held the sacred texts that monks, forbidden worldly possessions, kept for common use.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>By the ninth century the Benedictine Rule was the standard form of monastic life across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, except in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and Ireland. It is also followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cistercians">Cistercians</a> and Trappists, who form separate congregations, and by some Anglican, Lutheran and Eastern Orthodox communities. The English Reformation and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> swept away many abbeys, but the order revived in the nineteenth century, and Boniface Wimmer founded Saint Vincent Archabbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> in 1832.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Benedictines” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
